f sustained demand persistence Buffett looks for in a compounder. Operating margins are expanding by +11.7pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.", "Returns on capital run at 26% against a 8% cost of capital \u2014 a 18-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat Buffett teaches us to look for, with gross margins sitting at 33%.", "The business throws off $7.3B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 32 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.46\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard Buffett demands so the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 48\u00d7 is fortress-like.", "Shareholder yield of 2.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 3.1\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Price sits 28% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left", "sev": "amber", "cat": "valuation", "magnitude": 0.2795858472485413}, {"text": "Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies", "sev": "amber", "cat": "moat"}, {"text": "Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $4.2M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake", "sev": "amber", "cat": "insider"}, {"text": "Commodity price volatility and cost-curve position \u2014 in a downturn only the lowest-cost producer has a business, and the rest carry the overhead.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "Environmental remediation liability \u2014 tightening regulations on emissions, taili