ampbell's Company is a mid-cap consumer defensive business with a $6.3B market cap on $10.3B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +6% over the past three years and +2% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.", "Returns on capital run at 10% against a 7% cost of capital, a 3-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, with gross margins sitting at 31%.", "The business throws off $705M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 7 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.88\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 4.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 3.9\u00d7.", "Management returns a meaningful 8.2% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, which is Buffett\u2019s favourite signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 34% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 exactly the alignment Munger looks for, and within its sector the business captures 1.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Altman Z in the distress zone (1.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk Graham told investors to avoid entirely", "sev": "red", "cat": "quality"}, {"text": "Very elevated short interest (23.2% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business", "sev": "red", "cat": "sentiment"}, {"text": "Carries more leverage than most Consumer Defensive peers \u2014 a smaller cushion for the bad year Buffett always plans for", "sev": "amber", "cat": "balance_sheet"}, {"text": "Consumer Defensive sector underperforming the broader market this quarter", "sev": "amber", "cat": "sector"}, {"text": "Expansion environment