poration is a large-cap industrials business with a $15.8B market cap on $2.8B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +5% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.", "Returns on capital run at 13% against a 9% cost of capital, a 4-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it. Gross margins of 55% confirm the genuine pricing power Buffett prizes above all else.", "The business throws off $661M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 24 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.48\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 7.0\u00d7.", "Shareholder yield of 3.0% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 5.7% of the shares, and within its sector the business captures 2.0\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Goodwill represents 56% of total assets \u2014 a large impairment risk hanging over book value", "sev": "red", "cat": "balance_sheet"}, {"text": "Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled", "sev": "amber", "cat": "growth"}, {"text": "Price sits 23% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left", "sev": "amber", "cat": "valuation", "magnitude": 0.23435837704638882}, {"text": "Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $22.6M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake", "sev": "amber", "cat": "insider"}, {"text": "Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.