224237, "mc_p90_fv": 562.1840823943826, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Salesforce, Inc. is a large-cap technology business with a $171.9B market cap on $41.5B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +10% over the past three years and +29% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +5.2pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.", "Returns on capital run at 7% against a 10% cost of capital, which in Buffett\u2019s language means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests. Gross margins of 76% confirm the genuine pricing power Buffett prizes above all else.", "The business throws off $14.4B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 35 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.01\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably within Buffett\u2019s tolerance at 0.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA.", "Management returns a meaningful 8.2% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, which is Buffett\u2019s favourite signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 3.0% of the shares."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Goodwill represents 52% of total assets \u2014 a large impairment risk hanging over book value", "sev": "red", "cat": "balance_sheet"}, {"text": "Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled", "sev": "amber", "cat": "growth"}, {"text": "Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins", "sev": "amber", "cat": "moat"}, {"text": "Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies", "sev": "amber", "cat": "moat"}, {"text": "Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring", "sev": "amber", "cat": "quality"}, {"text": "Above-average short interest at 8.7% of float", "sev": "amber", "cat": "sentiment"}, {"text": "Antitrust and platform-regulation risk \u2014 the larger the network effect, the larger the regulatory target on its back, and a single consent decree can reset the moat.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "AI disruption of existing software moats \u2014 incumbents with durable margins face commoditisation as AI-native competitors replicate features at a fraction of the R&D cost.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engineering talent compresses margins even at scale.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}], "sector_tailwinds": ["Consistently beating estimates by 11% \u2014 management is under-promising and over-delivering", "Shares offer a 24% margin of safety against estimated fair value", "Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 Mr. Market is offering a discount", "Valued at a 33% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA", "Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency", "Founder-led \u2014 the alignment Buffett and Munger repeatedly say matters more than any incentive plan", "Insiders are net buyers (100% buy ratio) \u2014 the one signal Peter Lynch said there was only one reason for", "Large open-market insider purchases (net $1.0M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business", "Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment", "Analyst consensus points to 44% upside from current levels"], "news_headlines": [{"title": "Why Is Salesforce Stock Charging Higher On Monday?", "source": "benzinga.com", "link": "https://www.benzinga.com/trading-ideas/movers/26/04/51922031/why-is-salesforce-stock-charging-higher-on-monday", "date": "2026-04-20", "timestamp": 1776707140.0, "origin": "tiingo", "sen