v_durability": 100.0, "_score_sbc_dilution": 0.0, "_score_price_book": 62.8, "_score_fcf_margin": 100.0, "_score_valuation": 48.9, "_score_quality": 87.3, "_score_moat": 65.9, "_score_growth": 63.9, "_score_ownership": 28.8, "_composite_score": 51.9, "_composite_score_raw": 61.1, "_gates_passed": "13/20", "mc_confidence": "LOW (43%)", "mc_cv": 0.4321018385828611, "mc_p10_fv": 332.0531486075896, "mc_p90_fv": 872.9137207409499, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Intuit Inc. is a large-cap technology business with a $112.7B market cap on $18.8B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +14% over the past three years and +16% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence Buffett looks for in a compounder. Operating margins are expanding by +2.0pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.", "Returns on capital run at 13% against a 10% cost of capital, a 3-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it. Gross margins of 79% confirm the genuine pricing power Buffett prizes above all else.", "The business throws off $6.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 32 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.60\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably within Buffett\u2019s tolerance at 0.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 20\u00d7 is fortress-like.", "Shareholder yield of 3.5% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and stock-based comp at 10% of revenue is silent dilution the value investor has to account for, and insiders hold 2.4% of the shares."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Insiders are overwhelmingly selling (100% sell ratio) \u2014 the people who know the business want less of it", "s