oat": 80.4, "_score_growth": 61.1, "_score_ownership": 29.5, "_composite_score": 47.0, "_composite_score_raw": 55.3, "_gates_passed": "13/20", "mc_confidence": "LOW (63%)", "mc_cv": 0.6291775706439826, "mc_p10_fv": 99.47539368516748, "mc_p90_fv": 372.5145636502264, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Lam Research Corporation is a mega-cap technology business with a $330.5B market cap on $18.4B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +2% over the past three years and +13% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.", "Returns on capital run at 59% against a 13% cost of capital \u2014 a 46-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat Buffett teaches us to look for, with gross margins sitting at 47%.", "The business throws off $5.4B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 29 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.15\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard Buffett demands so the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and interest coverage of 33\u00d7 is fortress-like.", "Shareholder yield is only 1.4%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Price sits 90% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion Graham demanded has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)", "sev": "red", "cat": "valuation", "magnitude": 0.8963258719884245}, {"text": "Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled", "sev": "amber", "cat": "growth"}, {"text": "Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $29.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing the