h_conv": 37.7, "_score_rev_durability": 29.0, "_score_sbc_dilution": 90.3, "_score_price_book": 33.8, "_score_fcf_margin": 30.2, "_score_valuation": 8.5, "_score_quality": 29.9, "_score_moat": 69.7, "_score_growth": 72.3, "_score_ownership": 50.4, "_composite_score": 43.5, "_composite_score_raw": 46.7, "_gates_passed": "11/20", "mc_confidence": "MEDIUM (31%)", "mc_cv": 0.3148063490429184, "mc_p10_fv": 25.995640290479166, "mc_p90_fv": 54.388618606748864, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Coca-Cola Company (The) is a mega-cap consumer defensive business with a $324.9B market cap on $47.9B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.", "Returns on capital run at 19% against a 6% cost of capital \u2014 a 13-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat Buffett teaches us to look for. Gross margins of 60% confirm the genuine pricing power Buffett prizes above all else.", "The business throws off $5.3B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 11 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.57\u00d7, however, which raises Graham\u2019s oldest question: are these earnings opinions or facts?.", "On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 9.0\u00d7.", "Shareholder yield of 2.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 9.9% of the shares, and within its sector the business captures 3.8\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Price sits 43% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion Graham demanded has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)", "sev": "red", "cat": "valuation", "magnitude": 0.43388595508566297}, {"text": "Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $25.8M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake", "sev": "amber", "cat": "insider"}, {"text": "Consumer Defensive sector underperforming the broader market this quarter", "sev": "amber", "cat": "sector"}, {"text": "Expansion environment favors cyclical sectors over defensives", "sev": "amber", "cat": "sector"}, {"text": "Slow-motion taste and channel shifts \u2014 private label, Gen-Z health preferences, or a DTC disruption can erode decade-old brand moats without a single dramatic quarter.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "Input cost inflation from agricultural commodities \u2014 when raw materials spike, pass-through pricing lags and margin compression is immediate.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "GLP-1 and health-trend demand destruction \u2014 weight-loss drugs and wellness shifts are structurally reducing consumption in snack, beverage, and packaged food categories.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}], "sector_tailwinds": ["Reinvestment rate supports 15% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business", "Recent earnings have come in above analyst expectations", "Operating margins 17% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power", "Returns on capital have been remarkably steady for 5 years \u2014 the durability Munger calls the real test of a moat", "Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency", "Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet by Graham\u2019s test", "65 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 the operating record Graham looked for before any dividend claim could be trusted", "Gross margin tops the Consumer Defensive peer set \u2014 the pricing power Buffett prizes i