800941314662, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Ares Management Corporation is a large-cap financial services business with a $39.3B market cap on $5.6B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +22% over the past three years and +19% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence Buffett looks for in a compounder.", "Returns on capital run at 4% against a 10% cost of capital, which in Buffett\u2019s language means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests. Gross margins of 55% confirm the genuine pricing power Buffett prizes above all else.", "The business throws off $3.2B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 57 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 6.19\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "Accruals are elevated at -9.6% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.", "On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 11.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and Munger reminds us that liquor, ladies, and leverage are the three things that ruin people, and thin 1.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.", "Shareholder yield of 4.5% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and stock-based comp at 13% of revenue is silent dilution the value investor has to account for, and insiders hold 11% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 exactly the alignment Munger looks for, and profit capture is only 0.5\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Price sits 80% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion Graham demanded has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)", "sev": "red", "cat": "valuation", "magnit