e_quality": 48.9, "_score_moat": 19.7, "_score_growth": 52.0, "_score_ownership": 66.5, "_composite_score": 37.6, "_composite_score_raw": 37.6, "_gates_passed": "14/20", "mc_confidence": "MEDIUM (26%)", "mc_cv": 0.25744086107569014, "mc_p10_fv": 236.35523793287862, "mc_p90_fv": 408.76349369422707, "macro_regime": "expansion", "macro_composite": 0.475, "macro_erp": 0.053575, "financial_summary": ["Willis Towers Watson Public Lim is a large-cap financial services business with a $28.0B market cap on $9.7B of annual revenue.", "The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +10% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.", "Returns on capital run at 5% against a 7% cost of capital, which in Buffett\u2019s language means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 43%.", "The business throws off $1.5B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 16 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.11\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.", "On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably within Buffett\u2019s tolerance at 1.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 8.7\u00d7.", "Management returns a meaningful 7.2% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, which is Buffett\u2019s favourite signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."], "sector_headwinds": [{"text": "Altman Z in the distress zone (1.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk Graham told investors to avoid entirely", "sev": "red", "cat": "quality"}, {"text": "Revenue growth in the bottom decile of Financial Services peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool", "sev": "red", "cat": "growth"}, {"text": "Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled", "sev": "amber", "cat": "growth"}, {"text": "Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins", "sev": "amber", "cat": "moat"}, {"text": "Price sits 22% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left", "sev": "amber", "cat": "valuation", "magnitude": 0.2244318777308165}, {"text": "Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies", "sev": "amber", "cat": "moat"}, {"text": "Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and Buffett warns the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}, {"text": "Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.", "sev": "amber", "cat": "thesis_risk"}], "sector_tailwinds": ["Recent earnings have come in above analyst expectations", "Valued at a 34% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA", "24 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 the operating record Graham looked for before any dividend claim could be trusted", "Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment", "Analysts see meaningful upside (22%) to their mean target", "Broad market in a strong uptrend, trading well above its 200-day average", "Macro indicators collectively point to economic expansion"], "news_headlines": [{"title": "Is Willis Towers Watson\u2019s New Data Center Insurance Deepening Its Digital Moat or Stretching Focus (WTW)?", "source": "simplywall.st", "link": "https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/insurance/nasdaq-wtw/willis-towers-watson/news/is-willis-towers-watsons-new-data-center-insurance-deepening", "date": "2026-04-19", "timestamp": 1776557747.