whether customer value grows fast enough to justify current multiples.", "NVDA converts revenue into profit 12.6x more efficiently than HPE \u2014 the pool is being drained toward the efficient operator, and weak hands eventually exit."], "stats": {"total_revenue": 2315786363000.0, "total_op_income": 653830210000.0, "weighted_margin": 0.28233615174803584, "company_count": 81, "hhi": 0.0666, "cr4": 0.4436}}, "Energy": {"education": "Energy profit pools are dictated by commodity prices the producer does not set \u2014 oil, gas, refined products \u2014 and by position on the cost curve. In an upcycle every barrel is profitable; in a downcycle only the lowest-cost operators survive. Integrated majors smooth the cycle across upstream, midstream, and downstream, while pure E&P names offer leverage to the commodity with more extreme outcomes.", "education_cycle": "Energy runs on its own commodity cycle that only loosely tracks the broader economy: it tends to outperform late-cycle when inflation and demand both run hot, and collapses in recessions as demand destruction meets fixed supply. The sector is a natural inflation hedge and is often the last to participate in early-cycle recoveries.", "overview": "The Energy profit pool in this universe totals roughly $137.1B of operating income on $1.2T of revenue across 22 companies, a blended operating margin of 11.1%. XOM alone captures 25% of the pool on 26% of the revenue, setting the pace for the group. The top three names together account for 45% of profits from 46% of the revenue \u2014 roughly proportional to their revenue footprint.", "concentration": "HHI of 0.131 flags the sector as competitive with the top 4 capturing 63% of revenue \u2014 fragmentation keeps pricing honest and makes the pool hard to defend; margin discipline matters more than scale.", "key_players": [{"ticker": "XOM", "company_name": "Exxon Mobil Corporation", "role": "leader", "role_label": "Profit Leader", "note": "Captures 25% of the sector's oper