ss services \u2014 capital-goods businesses that live on order backlogs and installed-base service revenue. Margins are thinner than tech but the installed base acts like an annuity: once a customer standardizes on a supplier, switching costs are real. The best operators earn premium returns through aftermarket services, not equipment sales.", "education_cycle": "Early-cycle: industrials rip out of recessions as inventory rebuilds, capex thaws, and order books refill. The sector peaks mid-expansion and rolls over first when PMIs weaken \u2014 the ISM manufacturing index is the single best leading indicator for the group. Defense is the one counter-cyclical pocket, tied to government budgets rather than private capex.", "overview": "The Industrials profit pool in this universe totals roughly $207.3B of operating income on $1.6T of revenue across 73 companies, a blended operating margin of 12.7%. CAT alone captures 5% of the pool on 4% of the revenue, setting the pace for the group. The top three names together account for 14% of profits from 11% of the revenue \u2014 roughly proportional to their revenue footprint.", "concentration": "HHI of 0.028 flags the sector as competitive with the top 4 capturing 22% of revenue \u2014 fragmentation keeps pricing honest and makes the pool hard to defend; margin discipline matters more than scale.", "key_players": [{"ticker": "CAT", "company_name": "Caterpillar, Inc.", "role": "leader", "role_label": "Profit Leader", "note": "Captures 5% of the sector's operating income \u2014 the center of gravity for the profit pool.", "rating": "HOLD", "profit_share": 0.05240292333779863, "revenue_share": 0.041282353409871395, "op_margin": 0.16498246756128956, "pp_multiple": 1.2693782938563793}, {"ticker": "UNP", "company_name": "Union Pacific Corporation", "role": "runnerup", "role_label": "Runner-Up", "note": "Holds 5% of sector profits, the clear number-two position.", "rating": "HOLD", "profit_share": 0.04627021640964625, "revenue_share":